; A METHOD CALL ON A `pointer[T]` RECEIVER WAS SILENTLY DROPPED — rc 0, no
; diagnostic, and the whole body came out `ret void`, even for a method name
; that exists NOWHERE in the tree. report_unresolved_method_call# reports it.
;
; The mechanism is the call DETECTION, not the lookup: it accepts a member_access
; chain whose last hop is_method, or a PlannedType head. resolve_member_access#
; records an unresolved hop as a placeholder with is_method=false and a VOID
; result, so `is_call` stayed false, the operand pair collapsed, and the s185
; not-found gate — which lives INSIDE `if is_call` — was never reached.
;
; ★The receiver's type decided whether anything was said, and only by accident:
; for a CONCEPT the emitter later tripped over the dangling member READ (exit 16,
; no file and no line); for a PRIMITIVE pointee map_type answers `i8`, the
; emitter's struct-shaped trap does not fire, and nothing at all was said.
; `pointer[char]` is therefore the shape this pins.
;
; It was live: tscaly's get_property_name_for_known_symbol_name wrote its
; fallback name with three `buf.put(w, …)` into a `buf: pointer[char]` and had
; emitted NOTHING since the first port. The write on a raw buffer is
; `set *(buf + i): v`; method_on_pointer_receiver_ok.scaly is the positive twin.
; xfail: method not found: put in type char

procedure fill(q: pointer[char])
{
    q.put(0, "A" as char)
}

function main() returns int
{
    var buf char[8]
    fill(&buf[0])
    0
}
